and we're like, um, hi.


00:14:04.469 --> 00:14:06.638
- What, what, yeah?
- Yes.


00:14:08.389 --> 00:14:10.180
You do..


00:14:10.182 --> 00:14:11.932
- Close-up.
- Close-up?


00:14:11.934 --> 00:14:13.685
- And stage.
- Cards?


00:14:13.687 --> 00:14:15.522
- Close-up and stage.
- Oh, and stage.


00:14:42.756 --> 00:14:44.549
When did you start?


00:14:44.551 --> 00:14:47.468
I started around 3 or 4.


00:14:47.470 --> 00:14:49.389
3 or 4? Oh, really?


00:14:50.306 --> 00:14:52.559
Young. Me, I was 7.


00:14:55.269 --> 00:14:56.810
- I can't remember...
- 7.


00:14:56.812 --> 00:14:59.022
- 7.
- Oh, that's right, yeah.


00:14:59.024 --> 00:15:02.527
And that's, yeah,
that's, uh, 3, 4, 5.


00:15:14.163 --> 00:15:17.581
Alright, ready, I'm about
to head onto it.


00:15:17.583 --> 00:15:19.543
- There you go.
- Got it.


00:15:22.922 --> 00:15:24.254
That's the bottom
right there.


00:15:24.256 --> 00:15:27.925
But that, there must be a
base to get to the ground.


00:15:27.927 --> 00:15:29.426
It has a base
and then a bottom?


00:15:29.428 --> 00:15:31.094
That's the bottom.


00:15:31.096 --> 00:15:33.057
How can the bottom
be so far up in the..


00:15:34.808 --> 00:15:37.768
- Oh, we're underneath it?
- No.


00:15:37.770 --> 00:15:39.520
We're nowhere
near underneath it.


00:15:39.522 --> 00:15:42.982
Gee-wiz. Now what's it...
is it covered with metal?


00:15:42.984 --> 00:15:44.776
Yeah,
the whole thing's metal.


00:15:44.778 --> 00:15:46.694
It's not solid metal,
it's hollow, I'm guessing


00:15:46.696 --> 00:15:49.321
'cause there's viewing
windows at the top.


00:15:49.323 --> 00:15:51.616
This is the bot.. This is
the base of it here, right?


00:15:51.618 --> 00:15:53.242
Yeah, this is the base.


00:15:53.244 --> 00:15:54.285
At the back
of your hand.


00:15:54.287 --> 00:15:55.786
Well,
this is solid here.


00:15:55.788 --> 00:15:58.247
And we're almost
to the end.


00:15:58.249 --> 00:16:01.041
And there you go. Here,
let me see the cards.


00:16:01.043 --> 00:16:03.460
I'm gonna take the cards from you.
Don't forget, okay?


00:16:03.462 --> 00:16:05.921
So you have the triangle,
it goes..


00:16:05.923 --> 00:16:07.964
So this is the inside
and that's the...


00:16:07.966 --> 00:16:09.675
That's the point
I was just at over there?


00:16:09.677 --> 00:16:11.551
Point's the inside
and it goes up


00:16:11.553 --> 00:16:12.969
and around that way.


00:16:12.971 --> 00:16:14.137
Oh, I got ya.


00:16:14.139 --> 00:16:15.681
So the point's
at the bottom like that.


00:16:15.683 --> 00:16:18.309
So the point stays down
pointing all the way around.


00:16:18.311 --> 00:16:19.687
Well,
that's interesting.


00:16:25.777 --> 00:16:27.526
I never saw
a blind person


00:16:27.528 --> 00:16:29.322
before I started
losing my vision.


00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:34.494
I never even thought
about bein' blind.


00:16:46.339 --> 00:16:48.675
It was 1963.


00:16:51.177 --> 00:16:52.634
The teacher
was writing somethin'


00:16:52.636 --> 00:16:55.095
on the chalkboard.


00:16:55.097 --> 00:16:57.223
And I'm sitting
there looking


00:16:57.225 --> 00:17:00.017
and it's just a fuzz.


00:17:00.019 --> 00:17:03.023
And the day before,
I could see it just fine.


00:17:04.857 --> 00:17:07.691
If you could imagine,
you're looking at things


00:17:07.693 --> 00:17:09.235
the way they are.


00:17:09.237 --> 00:17:11.528
Then the next day,
you're lookin' at things


00:17:11.530 --> 00:17:14.364
in the way
you'd see a cartoon.


00:17:14.366 --> 00:17:16.286
There's no detail
on anything.


00:17:17.871 --> 00:17:19.996
The camera lens
got twisted


00:17:19.998 --> 00:17:22.206
and it became a blur.


00:17:22.208 --> 00:17:23.915
You couldn't focus
in on anything.


00:17:23.917 --> 00:17:25.543
I'm tryin' to,
tryin' to see the board.


00:17:25.545 --> 00:17:27.672
Well, where'd
the letters go?


00:17:29.298 --> 00:17:31.048
They just thought
that he had bad eyes.


00:17:31.050 --> 00:17:32.883
That he just needed glasses,
you know, and they could..


00:17:32.885 --> 00:17:34.846
Corrective lenses
would fix it.


00:17:37.473 --> 00:17:41.225
After a few months,
there was a black hole.


00:17:41.227 --> 00:17:44.353
And in the center,
it was just black.


00:17:44.355 --> 00:17:45.896
It was just
like a... a hat


00:17:45.898 --> 00:17:48.857
in front of your face
wherever you looked.


00:17:48.859 --> 00:17:51.443
As far as
what caused it


00:17:51.445 --> 00:17:53.740
we didn't know,
the doctors didn't know.


00:17:56.076 --> 00:17:58.910
We were referred
to another physician.


00:17:58.912 --> 00:18:00.244
We had some
bizarre test.


00:18:00.246 --> 00:18:02.246
That was like
I was in a spaceship.


00:18:02.248 --> 00:18:04.709
They had these dots,
blinking lights goin'.


00:18:07.420 --> 00:18:10.006
And they diagnosed him,
you know, then we knew.


00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:13.466
He had an eye disease.


00:18:13.468 --> 00:18:16.427
And it, uh,
it's something


00:18:16.429 --> 00:18:18.390
they can't do
anything about.


00:18:20.474 --> 00:18:21.932
I cried and cried..


00:18:21.934 --> 00:18:25.268
I could cry right now
just thinking about it.


00:18:25.270 --> 00:18:28.482
Yeah, it was hard.


00:18:41.203 --> 00:18:42.953
Hi, eh, my name
is Guy Landien.


00:18:42.955 --> 00:18:44.622
I'm just going just
to introduce you.


00:18:44.624 --> 00:18:45.664
Everyone knows
who you are


00:18:45.666 --> 00:18:47.208
but I just have
to say it right now.


00:18:47.210 --> 00:18:49.001
I know,
I know who I am too.


00:18:49.003 --> 00:18:52.005
- Good, great.
- He just introduced me.


00:18:52.007 --> 00:18:54.172
Yeah, good, great.


00:19:01.223 --> 00:19:02.806
May I ask for a big round
of applause


00:19:02.808 --> 00:19:05.142
for Richard Turner!


00:19:08.647 --> 00:19:11.940
Everybody help
the ladies keep track


00:19:11.942 --> 00:19:13.651
of the queen
of diamonds.


00:19:13.653 --> 00:19:15.569
We'll go 100 a throw,
not too much.


00:19:15.571 --> 00:19:16.653
Warm ya up.


00:19:16.655 --> 00:19:18.488
It's called
Find The Little Lady.


00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:20.824
Pair of deuces,
pair of deuces


00:19:20.826 --> 00:19:24.036
keep your eye
on the queen.


00:19:24.038 --> 00:19:25.999
Tell 'em, Jennifer,
1, 2, 3?


00:19:27.083 --> 00:19:28.665
- 3. You lost me actually.
- 3.


00:19:28.667 --> 00:19:31.001
Oh, Jennifer.


00:19:31.003 --> 00:19:34.005
As soon as we put money
on the table, $300.


00:19:34.007 --> 00:19:36.423
How 'bout this?
How 'bout this?


00:19:36.425 --> 00:19:37.842
What's that card,
Jennifer?


00:19:37.844 --> 00:19:39.052
- That's a queen.
- What's in the corner?


00:19:39.054 --> 00:19:40.678
- A bend.
- Yeah.


00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:44.181
This is so if you go to a
place called New York.


00:19:44.183 --> 00:19:46.267
They do this on the streets
and I won't move or blink


00:19:46.269 --> 00:19:47.727
until, Jennifer,
when you're ready


00:19:47.729 --> 00:19:49.478
you tell me to go. When
you're ready, say go.


00:19:49.480 --> 00:19:51.189
- Go.
- Where is it, Jen?


00:19:51.191 --> 00:19:52.648
- In the middle.
- Good job, Jen..


00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:54.611
Oh, Jennifer.


00:19:56.904 --> 00:19:59.029
Thank you, thank you,
thank you!


00:19:59.031 --> 00:20:00.366
Thank you very much.


00:20:07.207 --> 00:20:09.123
Rick, I need you
to get out your green pen.


00:20:09.125 --> 00:20:10.917
Right here.


00:20:10.919 --> 00:20:12.751
- I got a green pen.
- He has a green pen.


00:20:12.753 --> 00:20:14.211
The green pen
shows up better


00:20:14.213 --> 00:20:16.381
on these
than the black one.


00:20:16.383 --> 00:20:18.048
- And it's Stefan?
- Stefan.


00:20:18.050 --> 00:20:19.717
- That's my name, yeah.
- S-T-E..


00:20:19.719 --> 00:20:21.554
- F-A-N.
- F-A-N.


00:20:23.722 --> 00:20:25.555
I've learned Swedish.


00:20:25.557 --> 00:20:27.392
Willkommen which
is the same in German


00:20:27.394 --> 00:20:30.143
which I already knew and
Tack which is thank you


00:20:30.145 --> 00:20:32.898
and my favorite which
is Hej, that's hello.


00:20:48.789 --> 00:20:50.248
She's just sitting
on a chair.


00:20:50.250 --> 00:20:51.623
She got her arms folded.


00:20:58.842 --> 00:21:00.093
That's his thigh.


00:21:08.434 --> 00:21:10.352
What's under his thigh?
Gum?


00:21:12.605 --> 00:21:13.813
Give me that. Give it.


00:21:13.815 --> 00:21:16.565
Spit it out.
Spit it out.


00:21:16.567 --> 00:21:18.026
No, you didn't.


00:21:18.028 --> 00:21:19.943
You didn't.
I know that trick.


00:21:19.945 --> 00:21:22.738
Give it to me.
Give it to me.


00:21:22.740 --> 00:21:24.489
No, it's in your pocket.


00:21:24.491 --> 00:21:26.784
- Oh.
- I... I know that.


00:21:26.786 --> 00:21:29.495
Give me the gum.
Give me the gum.


00:21:29.497 --> 00:21:31.205
When Richard and I
first started dating


00:21:31.207 --> 00:21:32.457
he had some vision.


00:21:32.459 --> 00:21:34.792
He had like
peripheral vision.


00:21:34.794 --> 00:21:37.461
And so we would go out and
do, you know, fun things


00:21:37.463 --> 00:21:39.255
like, you know,
play basketball


00:21:39.257 --> 00:21:40.882
or go swimming
or go sailing


00:21:40.884 --> 00:21:42.924
or whatever the case
would happen to be.


00:21:42.926 --> 00:21:44.552
And he would always be
kind of looking out


00:21:44.554 --> 00:21:46.054
of the side of his eye
like that


00:21:46.056 --> 00:21:48.555
and that's kind of how
he would look at me too.


00:21:48.557 --> 00:21:50.682
- Multiple steps.
- Grab this rail.


00:21:50.684 --> 00:21:53.561
And then come down
2 steps.


00:21:53.563 --> 00:21:55.604
And then duck your head
way down.


00:21:55.606 --> 00:21:57.273
You know, he needed help
every now and then


00:21:57.275 --> 00:21:59.941
like what does that say
or obviously, driving


00:21:59.943 --> 00:22:01.902
was never gonna
be a thing for him.


00:22:01.904 --> 00:22:05.698
But clearly, he needs a lot more
help today than he did then.


00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:08.450
"Smoked salmon,
famous warm fishcakes ..."


00:22:08.452 --> 00:22:11.621
Hold it, smoked fishcakes.
Say that again.


00:22:11.623 --> 00:22:14.332
"Served with rye bread
and Danish remoulade."


00:22:14.334 --> 00:22:16.793
Start
from the beginning.


00:22:16.795 --> 00:22:18.294
- That is the beginning.
- Okay.


00:22:18.296 --> 00:22:20.505
Start from..
Okay. Dark bread.


00:22:20.507 --> 00:22:22.006
- Served on rye bread.
- Rye bread.


00:22:22.008 --> 00:22:24.676
I remember when we were
first married


00:22:24.678 --> 00:22:27.219
other people had told him, Oh,
you need to learn Braille.


00:22:27.221 --> 00:22:29.514
He goes, "I'm not gonna learn Braille.
That's for blind people."


00:22:29.516 --> 00:22:31.682
Or they said, uh,
You need to get a cane.


00:22:31.684 --> 00:22:34.102
Oh, he said, "I'm not
carrying a cane."


00:22:34.104 --> 00:22:36.312
I mean, you know,
i... it was a weakness


00:22:36.314 --> 00:22:38.525
and it was a weakness that he
was not going to admit to.


00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:58.250
Back button.
2145 Mesa Court Dr.


00:22:58.252 --> 00:23:00.460
Mesa, AZ, 85,209.


00:23:00.462 --> 00:23:02.005
1.0 miles
to destination.


00:23:02.007 --> 00:23:03.881
Estimated time,
18 minutes.


00:23:03.883 --> 00:23:05.382
Okay. Left, left.


00:23:05.384 --> 00:23:09.094
Left, left. Left, left.
That's it, girl.


00:23:09.096 --> 00:23:12.390
I started losing
my vision about a year


00:23:12.392 --> 00:23:15.019
after Richard did
and I was in 3rd grade.


00:23:17.855 --> 00:23:20.314
For me, it went fast.


00:23:20.316 --> 00:23:22.025
I'm doing
my math problems


00:23:22.027 --> 00:23:25.236
and all of a sudden,
my eyes just went black.


00:23:25.238 --> 00:23:28.197
And then when I open
'em up, that was it.


00:23:28.199 --> 00:23:30.951
It was like
within 60 seconds.


00:23:33.663 --> 00:23:36.288
I am independent


00:23:36.290 --> 00:23:38.915
in a different way
than Rick.


00:23:38.917 --> 00:23:40.584
He doesn't use
a cane, hardly


00:23:40.586 --> 00:23:43.379
and he doesn't
use a dog.


00:23:43.381 --> 00:23:46.048
Yes, good girl.
Good girl.


00:23:46.050 --> 00:23:48.968
He doesn't want people to
know that he doesn't see.


00:23:48.970 --> 00:23:50.930
Because he thinks
it's embarrassing.


00:23:53.767 --> 00:23:54.893
$1.


00:24:03.734 --> 00:24:04.985
Is anyone here?


00:24:06.695 --> 00:24:09.697
I just don't care
what people think.


00:24:09.699 --> 00:24:11.199
It's more important
for me to be able


00:24:11.201 --> 00:24:13.785
to have as much
as freedom as possible.


00:24:13.787 --> 00:24:15.577
How're you doin'
today, sir?


00:24:15.579 --> 00:24:17.622
Very well, thank you.
How about yourself?


00:24:17.624 --> 00:24:20.958
Oh, really good. Sure
a beautiful day today.


00:24:20.960 --> 00:24:21.961
Yes, it is.


00:24:23.421 --> 00:24:25.004
It is so liberating


00:24:25.006 --> 00:24:27.882
and I am such a,
much happier person.


00:24:27.884 --> 00:24:31.304
I feel like I can do pretty
well anything I want.


00:24:34.641 --> 00:24:37.936
For example, I wouldn't even
cross a very simple street.


00:24:39.520 --> 00:24:40.687
You know, I thought
people were nuts


00:24:40.689 --> 00:24:43.021
to cross a street.


00:24:43.023 --> 00:24:45.818
And now I can go
and get my groceries.


00:24:47.153 --> 00:24:49.072
I can go
and walk to the mall.


00:24:50.490 --> 00:24:52.784
I didn't wanna be
a burden on people.


00:24:55.494 --> 00:24:58.036
Okay, we're here.


00:24:58.038 --> 00:25:01.623
I think Rick is resistant
because he still


00:25:01.625 --> 00:25:03.961
has that mindset
that it's a weakness.


00:25:14.138 --> 00:25:16.889
When we both started
losin' our vision


00:25:16.891 --> 00:25:19.352
I think he struggled
way harder than I did.


00:25:21.563 --> 00:25:24.146
I can remember walkin' down
the street with my friend


00:25:24.148 --> 00:25:26.606
and we would go
blind bat, blind batty


00:25:26.608 --> 00:25:29.026
even though it was me
doin' it and my friend


00:25:29.028 --> 00:25:33.532
and I remember him just going
berserk from that kind of thing.


00:25:35.701 --> 00:25:38.411
And then
Rick and I both went


00:25:38.413 --> 00:25:40.373
to the school
for the blind.


00:25:41.374 --> 00:25:43.790
And for a stage there


00:25:43.792 --> 00:25:46.211
Rick was
self-destructive.


00:25:56.013 --> 00:25:58.055
When I was, uh, 11,
I was sent off


00:25:58.057 --> 00:26:01.476
to Callum Hills
and they had a VH room.


00:26:01.478 --> 00:26:03.813
VH stood
for Visually Handicapped.


00:26:05.565 --> 00:26:07.565
I did not like
the word handicapped.


00:26:07.567 --> 00:26:09.734
I still to this day, I can't
stand the word handicapped


00:26:09.736 --> 00:26:11.611
and I hated
the word blind.


00:26:11.613 --> 00:26:14.572
And so there was,
um, some hurt


00:26:14.574 --> 00:26:16.074
and a little bit
of rebellion


00:26:16.076 --> 00:26:19.703
that was starting
to fester within my soul.


00:26:21.998 --> 00:26:24.539
The teasing
from the other kids


00:26:24.541 --> 00:26:26.793
that's what really
ticked me off.


00:26:26.795 --> 00:26:29.379
The kids would
call me Mr. Magoo.


00:26:29.381 --> 00:26:31.672
They liked to say, How many
fingers am I holding up?


00:26:31.674 --> 00:26:33.924
And they would always
use the middle finger


00:26:33.926 --> 00:26:35.593
and they'd put it
right up next to my nose.


00:26:35.595 --> 00:26:37.844
How many fingers
am I holding up?


00:26:37.846 --> 00:26:41.100
It was embarrassing.
I wanted to beat 'em up.


00:26:42.769 --> 00:26:44.643
But I was crying.
I hate to admit it.


00:26:44.645 --> 00:26:46.271
I was weeping.
I was cryin'.


00:26:47.606 --> 00:26:51.735
And I was very mad
and, uh, and very hurt.


00:26:55.448 --> 00:26:57.448
My mom at first
was comforting.


00:26:57.450 --> 00:26:59.534
Then I think she just
didn't want to accept it


00:26:59.536 --> 00:27:01.328
so she kind
of withdrew.


00:27:02.913 --> 00:27:05.331
And I remember
one time she's ironing


00:27:05.333 --> 00:27:07.709
and I'm sitting there
trying to read


00:27:07.711 --> 00:27:10.503
my large print book
and she said


00:27:10.505 --> 00:27:13.299
"Must you put the book
so close to your eyes?"


00:27:15.009 --> 00:27:17.302
It was like I was broken
in her eyes.


00:27:19.346 --> 00:27:21.471
I went out
in my dad's workshop


00:27:21.473 --> 00:27:23.056
and I took
a magnifying glass


00:27:23.058 --> 00:27:26.479
that I was given to help me see...
see the large print.


00:27:28.647 --> 00:27:31.065
And I literally stared
at the sun trying


00:27:31.067 --> 00:27:32.986
to burn the eyes
out of my head.


00:27:35.113 --> 00:27:36.696
And I was cursing
everybody around me


00:27:36.698 --> 00:27:38.408
and I was cursing God.


00:27:40.660 --> 00:27:44.453
I was hurtin' inside and I couldn't
do what I was able to do before.


00:27:44.455 --> 00:27:45.912
And I thought
I just wanted to burn


00:27:45.914 --> 00:27:47.834
the worthless things
out of my head.


00:27:56.343 --> 00:27:58.051
My teacher
in the VH room


00:27:58.053 --> 00:28:00.680
she really did something
that was special for me.


00:28:02.015 --> 00:28:04.974
She had a giant 7 inch


00:28:04.976 --> 00:28:07.267
reel-to-reel
tape recorder.


00:28:07.269 --> 00:28:09.478
And she recorded
some pieces


00:28:09.480 --> 00:28:12.105
from a book called
"Expert At The Card Table"


00:28:12.107 --> 00:28:14.193
by S.W. Erdnase.


00:28:18.114 --> 00:28:19.654
It was a book
written in 1902


00:28:19.656 --> 00:28:21.742
and it was on how
to cheat at cards.


00:28:24.371 --> 00:28:27.747
At nighttime, I would
put on these headphones


00:28:27.749 --> 00:28:29.748
and I would listen
to this... this stuff


00:28:29.750 --> 00:28:32.045
while I slept
to get it in my head.


00:28:36.048 --> 00:28:38.716
There was somethin'
tactile about cards


00:28:38.718 --> 00:28:40.929
that my fingers liked.


00:28:43.765 --> 00:28:45.597
I loved the feel
of them in my hands.


00:28:45.599 --> 00:28:47.225
I loved the feel
of 5 cards


00:28:47.227 --> 00:28:49.184
6 cards, a poker hand


00:28:49.186 --> 00:28:50.436
where I'm actually
holding 6


00:28:50.438 --> 00:28:52.232
pretending
I'm holding 5.


00:28:54.983 --> 00:28:56.901
I didn't have to see
what I was doing.


00:28:56.903 --> 00:28:58.822
I could feel
what I was doin'.


00:29:21.510 --> 00:29:24.136
So you know how we had


00:29:24.138 --> 00:29:26.139
where we were
on the beach?


00:29:26.141 --> 00:29:27.431
- Just now?
- Yeah.


00:29:27.433 --> 00:29:29.519
So it's right over here,
right in the back of us.


00:29:30.936 --> 00:29:32.186
So you have the beach,
then you have a bit


00:29:32.188 --> 00:29:33.938
of flat land
for a little bit


00:29:33.940 --> 00:29:36.274
and then
it's just mountains.


00:29:36.276 --> 00:29:37.816
And they are all
covered with trees


00:29:37.818 --> 00:29:39.110
so they're
green mountains.


00:29:39.112 --> 00:29:41.072
- Green.
- Green mountains, yeah.


00:29:43.407 --> 00:29:45.533
I've been fishing
all my life.


00:29:45.535 --> 00:29:47.492
And I can feel
a fish fart


00:29:47.494 --> 00:29:51.122
within 3 feet
of my, my hook.


00:29:51.124 --> 00:29:53.333
I don't like to have my
pole sitting in the thing.


00:29:53.335 --> 00:29:55.668
I like to have it
in my hand the whole time.


00:29:55.670 --> 00:29:57.461
So I can feel
what's goin' on


00:29:57.463 --> 00:30:00.508
and feel the exact moment
that somethin' hits.


00:30:03.303 --> 00:30:04.387
Okay, yeah.


00:30:05.805 --> 00:30:07.766
That's a,
that's a fish, isn't it?


00:30:12.311 --> 00:30:14.728
- Woo! Is it on board?
- Oh, yeah.


00:30:14.730 --> 00:30:16.980
Alrighty, spademan.


00:30:16.982 --> 00:30:18.942
- Give me five!
- Over here.


00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:23.112
Don't bite the meat.


00:30:23.114 --> 00:30:25.030
Just bite the fin.
You can have the eyeball.


00:30:25.032 --> 00:30:26.407
I don't care about that.


00:30:26.409 --> 00:30:28.495
- He's gonna eat it.
- Alright, hold it up!


00:30:31.205 --> 00:30:32.498
Un eyeball.


00:30:40.298 --> 00:30:41.840
What is the point
of this?


00:30:43.425 --> 00:30:47.761
Well, when have you ever
chomped on an eyeball


00:30:47.763 --> 00:30:49.181
to see
what it tastes like?


00:30:51.601 --> 00:30:53.058
Okay, I need some water
to rinse my hands off.


00:30:53.060 --> 00:30:55.143
Yeah, David's got it.


00:30:55.145 --> 00:30:57.440
My dad is a little bit
on the crazy side.


00:30:59.150 --> 00:31:01.275
As a kid, he would
tell me some wild stories


00:31:01.277 --> 00:31:03.694
from when
he was younger.


00:31:03.696 --> 00:31:05.404
I used to think
he'd make them up


00:31:05.406 --> 00:31:07.699
'cause he would tell them
as bedtime stories to me.


00:31:07.701 --> 00:31:08.990
So I thought
they were like


00:31:08.992 --> 00:31:10.660
stuff out
of his imagination.


00:31:10.662 --> 00:31:13.412
Then I met all his friends
that he did them with.


00:31:13.414 --> 00:31:16.207
They're real
and it's kind of scary.


00:31:16.209 --> 00:31:17.499
They always say
back in the crazy days.


00:31:17.501 --> 00:31:19.209
Not the good old days,
the crazy days.


00:31:19.211 --> 00:31:20.504
- There?
- Yeah.


00:31:22.089 --> 00:31:23.881
Good. Little bit more
to the right.


00:31:23.883 --> 00:31:27.134
I didn't know much about Rick's,
uh, vision in high school.


00:31:27.136 --> 00:31:30.513
I mean, really, uh, Rick
seemed to get along okay


00:31:30.515 --> 00:31:32.222
just as if
he were sighted.


00:31:32.224 --> 00:31:34.808
And that was
kind of strange.


00:31:34.810 --> 00:31:36.768
He just,
he refused to be like


00:31:36.770 --> 00:31:38.854
any old
other blind person.


00:31:38.856 --> 00:31:40.565
He actually did stuff


00:31:40.567 --> 00:31:43.276
that, uh, that he really
shouldn't have done.


00:31:43.278 --> 00:31:45.111
- Okay.
- You get what I'm saying?


00:31:45.113 --> 00:31:46.698
Oh, Juda! Juda!


00:31:51.326 --> 00:31:53.369
At that time,
I could see houses


00:31:53.371 --> 00:31:57.707
trees within like a 40,
50 foot circumference.


00:31:57.709 --> 00:32:00.084
I could see well enough
to ride a bicycle


00:32:00.086 --> 00:32:01.835
and so I would follow
the lines.


00:32:01.837 --> 00:32:03.086
I figured
if I could ride a bike


00:32:03.088 --> 00:32:05.672
I should be able
to ride a motorcycle.


00:32:05.674 --> 00:32:07.467
I'd jump on the back
and he'd say


00:32:07.469 --> 00:32:08.884
"Is there anybody
coming on the left?


00:32:08.886 --> 00:32:10.053
Anybody comin'
on the right?"


00:32:10.055 --> 00:32:12.596
I'd say nope, go.


00:32:12.598 --> 00:32:14.181
I guess he could watch
one of the lines


00:32:14.183 --> 00:32:16.603
and you know, on down
the highway we went.


00:32:18.355 --> 00:32:21.357
That was part of his lifestyle
was being not blind.


00:32:23.067 --> 00:32:25.442
I just had
so much energy.


00:32:25.444 --> 00:32:27.944
If there was an obstacle,
if there was a cliff


00:32:27.946 --> 00:32:30.238
whatever it was,
I had to overcome it.


00:32:30.240 --> 00:32:32.283
I had to beat it.


00:32:32.285 --> 00:32:34.701
Rick joined
this karate class.


00:32:34.703 --> 00:32:36.787
He was very,
very determined.


00:32:36.789 --> 00:32:39.457
Rick said he did not wanna
be treated any differently.


00:32:39.459 --> 00:32:41.750
So he would go through
all the same drills.


00:32:41.752 --> 00:32:45.004
I told everybody, Don't knock him out.
Don't injure him.


00:32:45.006 --> 00:32:47.090
But then Rick started
hitting people


00:32:47.092 --> 00:32:49.174
and he liked it.


00:32:49.176 --> 00:32:52.804
You don't have to see where every
body part is. You just know.


00:32:52.806 --> 00:32:54.639
And I didn't care
where any body part was.


00:32:54.641 --> 00:32:56.641
I was just hittin'
whatever I could hit.


00:32:56.643 --> 00:32:58.851
It's a little bit exhausting
to be around Rick


00:32:58.853 --> 00:33:01.228
'cause he's just always
doin' something.


00:33:01.230 --> 00:33:03.815
He had so much potential
for so many things.


00:33:03.817 --> 00:33:06.359
But he was
all over the place.


00:33:06.361 --> 00:33:08.568
I was a perpetual
motion machine.


00:33:08.570 --> 00:33:10.321
I never stopped moving.


00:33:10.323 --> 00:33:12.573
My cards became
just the perfect thing


00:33:12.575 --> 00:33:15.367
because I was able
to develop something


00:33:15.369 --> 00:33:17.413
while I burned off
that nervous energy.


00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:23.000
You know, I'm a nervous person.
Always have to be movin'.


00:33:23.002 --> 00:33:24.919
And so before
I would scratch myself


00:33:24.921 --> 00:33:26.962
or wiggle or jerk
somewhere or another


00:33:26.964 --> 00:33:29.090
so I just funnel
all of my nervous energy


00:33:29.092 --> 00:33:31.428
into practicing moves
with the cards.


00:33:48.153 --> 00:33:50.611
In one of his early
literature pamphlets


00:33:50.613 --> 00:33:54.865
it said, "Richard Turner
practices 16 hours a day."


00:33:54.867 --> 00:33:57.367
And when I read that,
I thought


00:33:57.369 --> 00:33:59.411
Am I supposed
to believe that?


00:33:59.413 --> 00:34:00.912
You know,
nobody practices 6..


00:34:00.914 --> 00:34:02.373
That... that's the dumbest
thing I've ever heard.


00:34:02.375 --> 00:34:05.250
Who... who's gonna
believe that?


00:34:05.252 --> 00:34:08.713
Fast forward 15 years,
I'm stayin' at his house


00:34:08.715 --> 00:34:12.425
and he absolutely
practiced 16 hours a day.


00:34:12.427 --> 00:34:14.387
He never puts
the cards down.


00:34:15.972 --> 00:34:19.014
Rick always
shuffles cards


00:34:19.016 --> 00:34:22.894
and makes that little
tshh sound all the time.


00:34:22.896 --> 00:34:24.896
He does it at church.
He does it in the car.


00:34:24.898 --> 00:34:26.104
He does it
in the grocery store.


00:34:26.106 --> 00:34:27.690
He does it when
we're layin' in bed.


00:34:27.692 --> 00:34:29.400
He does it
when he's thinking.


00:34:29.402 --> 00:34:31.235
He does it
while he's working out.


00:34:31.237 --> 00:34:33.028
I've never been
in an environment


00:34:33.030 --> 00:34:34.988
except maybe
in the pool. No, no.


00:34:34.990 --> 00:34:36.615
When we first met,
we went to the Jacuzzi


00:34:36.617 --> 00:34:39.409
he took his cards
with him.


00:34:39.411 --> 00:34:41.996
She got so frustrated
and then she said to me


00:34:41.998 --> 00:34:46.501
"Do you know what? The
other night we were in bed


00:34:46.503 --> 00:34:50.921
we were making love
and I heard this tshh."


00:34:50.923 --> 00:34:54.258
He was practicing
shuffling with one hand


00:34:54.260 --> 00:34:57.260
while they were
making love.


00:34:57.262 --> 00:34:59.514
We all stayed
in a motel room together


00:34:59.516 --> 00:35:01.974
and I remember he had
laid down on the bed


00:35:01.976 --> 00:35:05.978
to go to sleep and he had a
deck of cards in each hand


00:35:05.980 --> 00:35:08.438
and he was shuffling the
cards while he was listening


00:35:08.440 --> 00:35:10.273
to the television
and then he drifted off


00:35:10.275 --> 00:35:12.401
to sleep
and his hands stopped.


00:35:12.403 --> 00:35:14.655
And the next morning, this
is exactly what happened.


00:35:18.492 --> 00:35:21.453
His hands started moving
before his eyes opened up.


00:35:30.213 --> 00:35:32.130
It was here
at the Magic Castle


00:35:32.132 --> 00:35:35.048
in this corner known
as the Dai Vernon Corner


00:35:35.050 --> 00:35:36.883
named after
Professor Dai Vernon


00:35:36.885 --> 00:35:39.554
where my, uh, destiny
with a pack of cards


00:35:39.556 --> 00:35:41.472
was really set.


00:35:41.474 --> 00:35:43.391
I'm 84 years of age


00:35:43.393 --> 00:35:46.936
and I've been studying
magic for 78 years.


00:35:46.938 --> 00:35:49.063
I wasted the first 6 years
of my life


00:35:49.065 --> 00:35:50.356
but... but..


00:35:51.568 --> 00:35:52.983
What do you mean
you've been studying?


00:35:52.985 --> 00:35:54.443
Where do you study it?
With other famous...


00:35:54.445 --> 00:35:56.862
No, you... you sit
in a room


00:35:56.864 --> 00:35:58.322
and you take
a pack of cards


00:35:58.324 --> 00:36:00.992
you take some dice,
you take a handkerchief


00:36:00.994 --> 00:36:04.287
and you try to create some
kind of magical effect


00:36:04.289 --> 00:36:05.749
and you work it out.


00:36:14.548 --> 00:36:18.175
Dai Vernon was the single
most important person


00:36:18.177 --> 00:36:19.844
in the development
of magic


00:36:19.846 --> 00:36:22.012
in the last
hundred years.


00:36:22.014 --> 00:36:25.016
Houdini had a boast
that any card trick


00:36:25.018 --> 00:36:28.394
if he saw it twice,
he could figure it out.


00:36:28.396 --> 00:36:31.229
Vernon, as a brash
young man, met Houdini


00:36:31.231 --> 00:36:33.358
and did a card trick
for him


00:36:33.360 --> 00:36:35.776
and Houdini kind of
knit his brow.


00:36:35.778 --> 00:36:37.695
And after the 2nd
or 3rd time


00:36:37.697 --> 00:36:39.363
Houdini's wife said


00:36:39.365 --> 00:36:42.032
"Oh Harry, come on,
admit it he's fooled you."


00:36:42.034 --> 00:36:44.659
He certainly
was an absolute master


00:36:44.661 --> 00:36:45.912
of sleight of hand.


00:36:45.914 --> 00:36:49.456
And he was also hooked
on gambling moves.


00:36:49.458 --> 00:36:51.208
He heard I could do
some difficult moves


00:36:51.210 --> 00:36:53.711
with the cards and
he agreed to meet with me


00:36:53.713 --> 00:36:56.007
at the world-famous Magic
Castle in Hollywood.


00:37:00.469 --> 00:37:02.804
When I first showed Professor
Vernon my second deal


00:37:02.806 --> 00:37:04.889
this is how
I was dealing.


00:37:04.891 --> 00:37:07.391
You know, I thought,
oh, totally hidin' it.


00:37:07.393 --> 00:37:08.726
Nobody can see
what I'm doing.


00:37:08.728 --> 00:37:12.688
And he goes, "Well,
that's unnatural.


00:37:12.690 --> 00:37:14.691
What's your name again?
Turner? Richard?


00:37:14.693 --> 00:37:16.275
That's unnatural,
Turner.


00:37:16.277 --> 00:37:18.610
You can't, it doesn't
work, it's unnatural."


00:37:18.612 --> 00:37:21.322
And all of a sudden, he grabbed
my hand here and he said


00:37:21.324 --> 00:37:23.199
"Now watch.
No movement at all."


00:37:23.201 --> 00:37:25.242
And then he said, "Feel, feel
my hands, feel my hands."


00:37:25.244 --> 00:37:27.494
And so I, uh, see
this blurry image


00:37:27.496 --> 00:37:28.912
out of the corner
of my eye


00:37:28.914 --> 00:37:30.873
as he was describin'
a move.


00:37:30.875 --> 00:37:32.166
One of the things


00:37:32.168 --> 00:37:34.251
Vernon would talk
about is naturalness.


00:37:34.253 --> 00:37:36.753
He said a lot of people
hold the cards like this.


00:37:36.755 --> 00:37:38.171
You know, it's called
a mechanic's grip.


00:37:38.173 --> 00:37:39.590
They really
hide the cards


00:37:39.592 --> 00:37:41.425
because they can get away
with dirty work.


00:37:41.427 --> 00:37:43.760
But he said if you hold the
cards with your fingers


00:37:43.762 --> 00:37:46.722
across the side,
more deck is exposed.


00:37:46.724 --> 00:37:49.766
And if you're not trying
to hide the move


00:37:49.768 --> 00:37:52.186
people are less inclined
to believe that


00:37:52.188 --> 00:37:53.898
you're doin' somethin'
dishonest.


00:37:55.233 --> 00:37:57.608
He said it takes
no effort


00:37:57.610 --> 00:37:59.110
to deal a card
off the top.


00:37:59.112 --> 00:38:01.737
A 4 year old
can take that card


00:38:01.739 --> 00:38:03.447
and deal it
on the table.


00:38:03.449 --> 00:38:06.700
So you need to be able
to do the second card


00:38:06.702 --> 00:38:09.288
with that same
natural ease.


00:38:23.260 --> 00:38:26.970
I would go home
and I would analyze it.


00:38:26.972 --> 00:38:31.224
I'd take it apart
piece by piece.


00:38:31.226 --> 00:38:35.938
And I would practice
that move in slow motion.


00:38:35.940 --> 00:38:38.983
Training my hands
to do exactly


00:38:38.985 --> 00:38:40.945
what I wanted
to accomplish.


00:38:42.655 --> 00:38:45.742
And then I would turn it
into a subconscious habit.


00:39:00.382 --> 00:39:02.798
10,000 times,
20,000 times in a day


00:39:02.800 --> 00:39:04.427
doing that same move.


00:39:05.719 --> 00:39:07.012
Like a machine.


00:39:10.182 --> 00:39:11.932
And then when I'd go back
to show professor


00:39:11.934 --> 00:39:13.311
he'd go, "That's it."


00:39:14.853 --> 00:39:17.063
He saw obsession in me.


00:39:17.065 --> 00:39:20.273
Obsessed like
he was obsessed.


00:39:20.275 --> 00:39:22.275
He started
unloading on me.


00:39:22.277 --> 00:39:24.153
He started
tipping things that


00:39:24.155 --> 00:39:25.655
he hadn't shared
with other people.


00:39:25.657 --> 00:39:28.365
I still have things
that I have today


00:39:28.367 --> 00:39:31.077
that nobody else knows
besides me and him.


00:39:35.125 --> 00:39:37.041
Just in case
anyone is skeptical


00:39:37.043 --> 00:39:38.418
about what
you've just seen


00:39:38.420 --> 00:39:39.876
Richard's going
to show us some more


00:39:39.878 --> 00:39:41.713
of his incredible
skills.


00:39:41.715 --> 00:39:42.880
How many people
in the card game, John?


00:39:42.882 --> 00:39:43.880
Give me
a number of players.


00:39:43.882 --> 00:39:44.882
Oh, 5.


00:39:44.884 --> 00:39:47.217
1, 2, 3, 4, 5.


00:39:47.219 --> 00:39:48.760
Another number,
wherever you say


00:39:48.762 --> 00:39:49.804
that's where
the ace will be.


00:39:49.806 --> 00:39:50.887
6.


00:39:50.889 --> 00:39:52.724
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.


00:39:52.726 --> 00:39:54.307
Remember that one
is facedown.


00:39:54.309 --> 00:39:56.019
These are coming out
of the middle.


00:39:56.021 --> 00:39:57.228
I want you sit
in the middle.


00:39:57.230 --> 00:39:58.311
I'll put it
a little further for you.


00:39:58.313 --> 00:40:00.565
- Okay.
- Another number, John.


00:40:00.567 --> 00:40:02.860
- Uh, 2.
- 1, 2.


00:40:05.070 --> 00:40:07.655
Slow motion.


00:40:07.657 --> 00:40:10.157
The first time I saw
Richard was on television.


00:40:10.159 --> 00:40:12.744
The show was called
"That's Incredible."


00:40:12.746 --> 00:40:16.414
I'll never forget that 'cause I
was dealing craps at the Aladdin


00:40:16.416 --> 00:40:17.999
on a strip in Las Vegas
at the time.


00:40:18.001 --> 00:40:19.708
And when I went back
into work the next day


00:40:19.710 --> 00:40:22.045
everybody was talkin' about
this guy on television


00:40:22.047 --> 00:40:24.380
who'd, uh, brought
the house down.


00:40:24.382 --> 00:40:26.340
What blows me away is,
if you list all


00:40:26.342 --> 00:40:27.883
of the different
gamblers' moves


00:40:27.885 --> 00:40:29.927
and you come up with things
like the second deal


00:40:29.929 --> 00:40:33.097
and push-throughs
and perfect shuffles.


00:40:33.099 --> 00:40:36.559
Most people have one good
move that they're known.


00:40:36.561 --> 00:40:38.894
Richard spent
his entire life mastering


00:40:38.896 --> 00:40:41.356
all of the things
on the top of the list.


00:40:41.358 --> 00:40:43.816
I'll show you the touch
you have to have.


00:40:43.818 --> 00:40:45.943
Give me a number
between 1 to 52.


00:40:45.945 --> 00:40:48.278
How about something
like 37?


00:40:48.280 --> 00:40:50.990
37. Try that.


00:40:50.992 --> 00:40:52.115
But you do that so fast.


00:40:52.117 --> 00:40:56.662
31, 32, 33, 34,
35, 36, 37.


00:40:59.333 --> 00:41:01.668
He's a trickster
and not only that


00:41:01.670 --> 00:41:05.338
uh, Richard Turner
is also legally blind.


00:41:05.340 --> 00:41:07.423
And he can do things with
cards no one else can do.


00:41:07.425 --> 00:41:09.633
- How do you do that?
- How do I do which?


00:41:09.635 --> 00:41:11.177
Well, how do you,
how do you..


00:41:11.179 --> 00:41:12.470
What I get angry about


00:41:12.472 --> 00:41:14.347
is if someone
told somebody


00:41:14.349 --> 00:41:15.639
Oh, this guy's blind.


00:41:15.641 --> 00:41:17.641
I need to tell you
about Richard's eyesight.


00:41:17.643 --> 00:41:19.644
Did you notice that Richard
brought it up very closely?


00:41:19.646 --> 00:41:21.813
Tell them
about your eyesight.


00:41:21.815 --> 00:41:24.816
It's the way people all of a
sudden perceive and treat you


00:41:24.818 --> 00:41:27.943
and I... I...
I didn't like it.


00:41:27.945 --> 00:41:30.320
We all hear about people
who have average


